  "content": {
    "id": "p3-q5-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "5",
    "prompt": "Suppose $\\cos\\alpha=\\frac{5}{13}$ and $\\sin\\beta=\\frac35$ where $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ are both acute. Find $\\cos(\\alpha+\\beta)$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$-\\frac{16}{65}$",
      "worked": "Both angles are acute, so the missing ratios are positive.\n$$\\begin{align*}\\sin\\alpha&=\\sqrt{1-\\left(\\frac{5}{13}\\right)^2}\\\\\\sin\\alpha&=\\frac{12}{13}\\\\\\cos\\beta&=\\sqrt{1-\\left(\\frac35\\right)^2}\\\\\\cos\\beta&=\\frac45\\end{align*}$$\nUsing the cosine addition formula:\n$$\\begin{align*}\\cos(\\alpha+\\beta)&=\\cos\\alpha\\cos\\beta-\\sin\\alpha\\sin\\beta\\\\\\cos(\\alpha+\\beta)&=\\frac{5}{13}\\times\\frac45-\\frac{12}{13}\\times\\frac35\\\\\\cos(\\alpha+\\beta)&=\\frac{20-36}{65}\\\\\\cos(\\alpha+\\beta)&=-\\frac{16}{65}\\end{align*}$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 52
  },
